---
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
import { BATHROBE_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { pageTitle } from '@/lib/seo';
---

<ProductLayout
  title={pageTitle('Hotel Bathrobes')}
  description="Cotton hotel bathrobes, about 1,200 g per robe, 16s cut pile, kimono collar. Wholesale from our Qingdao factory."
  h1="Hotel bathrobes"
  breadcrumb="Bathrobes"
  specs={BATHROBE_SPECS}
  images={['products-bathrobes-hero', 'products-bathrobes-detail']}
>
  <p>
    Our robe is 100% cotton in a 16s cut pile, about 1,200 g per robe, with a kimono collar and
    a belt at the waist. Cut pile is the terry face with the loops sheared off, which gives a
    flatter, softer surface than loop terry.
  </p>
  <p>
    A bathrobe is the only textile in the room a guest wears against bare skin. Weight, length
    and how the collar sits are noticed immediately, in a way a thread count is not. It is also
    the item most often taken home, so the replacement rate belongs in the budget from the start.
    We make the robes at our plant in Jimo, alongside the bed linen and towels.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Cut pile, loop terry and waffle</h2>
  <p>
    Three faces dominate hotel robes and they behave differently in a commercial laundry.
    Loop terry keeps the loops standing: thicker, more surface to absorb water, rougher on the
    skin, and slower to dry because the loops trap moisture. Cut pile shears them off after
    weaving, which leaves a flatter, softer surface that looks more like a garment and releases
    water more readily. Waffle is an open basket weave: lighter, fastest to dry, cooler
    on the skin. It suits spa and pool use and hot climates, but gives up the plush weight
    guests associate with a five-star room.
  </p>
  <p>
    The choice is usually driven by laundry turnaround rather than hand feel. If housekeeping
    washes and re-issues robes the same day, drying speed matters more than the showroom touch.
    If robes sit in a suite, a heavier cut pile is the better buy. Our line is cut pile, 16s
    yarn: softer against the skin than loop terry, and built for contract washing.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">1200 g is the weight of the robe</h2>
  <p>
    Weight is where robe quotes are hardest to compare. The number we give, about 1200 g, is the
    finished weight of the garment, not the fabric. Garment weight changes with size: an XL uses
    more cloth than an S and always weighs more. A supplier quoting one weight for every size is
    quoting the smallest. Ask for the finished weight at the size you order, and for the fabric
    weight in grams per square metre. Only the two numbers together compare honestly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heavier is not automatically better. More cotton means more water in the wash, a longer dry
    cycle and a higher freight cost, and a 1400 g robe that never dries properly is worse than a
    1100 g robe that does. Hotel robes generally sit between 1100 and 1400 g; around 1200 g reads
    substantial without becoming a laundry problem.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Kimono collar or shawl collar</h2>
  <p>
    A kimono collar is a flat band running down both fronts and sitting on the shoulders. It
    folds flat, stacks neatly and still looks tidy when a guest has tied the belt carelessly,
    which is why hotel programmes use it. A shawl collar rolls around the back of the neck,
    feels warmer and bulkier, uses more fabric and does not fold as flat. Hooded robes belong
    to spa and pool areas, not the guest room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Collar and length are quoted together because both change the cutting plan: a mid-calf and
    a knee-length robe in the same chest size are different garments, as are full and
    three-quarter sleeves. None of these is wrong, but each has to be decided before the fabric
    is cut. Send the length you use now, measured the way your current supplier measures it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Size is where robe orders go wrong</h2>
  <p>
    A robe one size too small stops above the knee, the belt sits high and the whole room reads
    cheap. S, M, L, XL on their own mean very little between suppliers. Ask for a size chart in
    centimetres, measured flat: chest, total length, sleeve length and belt length. Confirm
    whether length is measured from the high point of the shoulder or the base of the collar,
    and make sure both sides use the same point.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unisex and ladies' cuts are not the same garment with a different label. A unisex robe has a
    straighter body and a wider shoulder; a ladies' cut narrows at the waist and shortens the
    sleeve. If you use both, say so, because the size mix drives the cutting and the price.
    Approve a middle-size sample, then order the other sizes against that construction.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What to put in the brief</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>Size chart in centimetres and quantity per size, not just a total.</li>
    <li>Face: cut pile, loop terry or waffle, and the yarn number if you have one.</li>
    <li>Collar, sleeve length and total length.</li>
    <li>Colour. White is standard. Any other shade is a dye run with its own minimum.</li>
    <li>Logo: embroidery or woven label, placement, size, stitch count and number of colours.</li>
    <li>Fibre content and care label wording, if the property has a standard label.</li>
    <li>Packing: folded in a poly bag or on a hanger changes the carton count and the freight.</li>
    <li>Belt: attached or separate, and whether you want spare belts in the carton.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Logos can be embroidered or woven in. Placement matters more than most buyers expect:
    chest-left is read at a glance, a sleeve logo only when the guest raises an arm, a back logo
    only when the robe comes off. Send artwork with the colour count stated and we will confirm
    what the embroidery will hold at the size you specify.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Surviving a commercial laundry</h2>
  <p>
    Cotton takes up shrinkage in the first washes, robes included. Settle before ordering whether
    the chart is measured before or after washing. If your laundry has a standard wash test, send
    it and we will quote against that, so the size holds on the tenth wash, not just the first.
  </p>
  <p>
    In contract laundry the collar, cuffs, belt loops and hems wear first. Belt loops take the
    most load of any seam, and a robe without its belt is unusable, so ask for spare belts. White
    robes show every stain, so confirm bleach tolerance before committing to a programme that
    uses oxygen bleach. We would rather answer that with the sample in hand than with a promise.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Where robes sit in a hotel programme</h2>
  <p>
    Robes are usually placed in one of three tiers: suites only, all guest rooms, or spa and pool
    only. The tier decides the weight and the replacement budget. They wear out at the belt and
    collar first, so a programme that budgets the opening order but not the replacement will run
    short. Ordering them with
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/hotel-towels/">hotel towels</a> and
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/hotel-slippers/">hotel slippers</a> keeps it to one
    shipment, one inspection and one set of labels.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">How we quote robes</h2>
  <p>
    The robes are cut and sewn at our plant in Jimo, alongside the bed linen. Minimum order is
    50 pieces per style, trade accounts only, FOB Qingdao or CIF to your port. Send the brief
    above and we will come back with a price per size, the fabric weight behind it and a sample
    before bulk. Sheet and duvet construction is on the
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/hotel-bedding-supplier/">hotel bedding supplier</a>
    page; anything else goes through
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/request-a-quote/">request a quote</a>.
  </p>
</ProductLayout>
